# URL normalization

Drift is a set comparison over URLs. Every un-canonicalized variant registers
as fake churn and corrupts the headline finding. This module is the highest-risk
component in the project.

**Write the tests first.** Minimum 12 cases covering every rule below, including
at least 3 that SHOULD normalize to different URLs (over-normalization is as
bad as under-normalization).

Rules, in order:
1. Lowercase scheme and host. Preserve path case.
2. Strip `www.` prefix
3. Drop default ports (:80, :443)
4. Remove tracking params: utm_*, gclid, fbclid, msclkid, ref, source, mc_cid
5. PRESERVE content-bearing params: id, article, page, p, q, v
6. Remove fragments EXCEPT where the fragment is the content anchor
   (some medical reference sites route on fragment — flag any you find)
7. Normalize trailing slash: strip, except on bare host
8. Follow redirects to final URL. Cache aggressively — this is the slow step.
9. AMP → canonical: `/amp/` path segment, `?amp=1`, `amp.` subdomain
10. When the page is fetched, honor `<link rel="canonical">` over the URL

Persist BOTH `url_raw_first_seen` and `url_canonical`. The report shows drift
computed both ways so the normalization delta is visible and auditable.

Do not add normalization rules beyond this list without asking. Aggressive
normalization that collapses genuinely distinct pages hides real churn.
